# Example environment variables.
#
# Copy this to `.env` (git-ignored) and fill in real values. NEVER put a real
# secret in this file — it is committed.
#
# This app is a **Plaid sandbox demo**. It is not built to hold real financial
# data and must never be pointed at Plaid production. Several things below
# enforce or assume that; see PLAID_ENV.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The pooled connection the app queries through, and the unpooled one
# `prisma migrate` needs. Neon shows both on the project dashboard — take the
# pooled URL for DATABASE_URL and the direct URL for DIRECT_URL.
#
# `prisma.config.ts` runs migrations against DIRECT_URL, so a wrong value there
# is a migration pointed somewhere unintended. The integration suite refuses to
# run unless BOTH resolve to the local Docker Postgres; see
# `test/integration/support/guard.ts`.
DATABASE_URL=
DIRECT_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Encryption — Plaid access tokens at rest
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# AES-256-GCM keys in a registry, so they can be rotated:
#   ENCRYPTION_KEY_<id>       = base64-encoded 32 random bytes
#   ENCRYPTION_KEY_CURRENT_ID = the id new encryptions use
#
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# A sandbox access token is not a sensitive credential, so this is not strictly
# load-bearing here. It stays on anyway: handling tokens correctly is part of
# what this project exists to demonstrate, and a demo that skips the security
# step demonstrates the wrong thing.
ENCRYPTION_KEY_0=
ENCRYPTION_KEY_CURRENT_ID=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plaid — SANDBOX ONLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# From https://dashboard.plaid.com/developers/keys.
#
# Use the **sandbox** secret. The variable is named PLAID_SANDBOX_SECRET rather
# than PLAID_SECRET on purpose: it should be impossible to paste a production
# secret in here without noticing. The integration suite's `assertPlaidSandbox`
# refuses to run if a production credential is present at all.
PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
PLAID_SANDBOX_SECRET=
PLAID_ENV=sandbox

# Must be registered under "Allowed redirect URIs" in the Plaid dashboard, or
# Link rejects it. `lib/plaid/link-token.ts` falls back to VERCEL_URL and then
# to http://localhost:3000 when this is unset.
#
# Do not set it to an empty string on a deploy platform: an unset secret expands
# to '', and `??` does not fall back on '', so Plaid would receive
# `redirect_uri: ""` and reject it. Leave the variable out entirely instead.
PLAID_REDIRECT_URI=

# Where Plaid POSTs item updates. Plaid has to be able to reach it, so a
# localhost value only works behind a tunnel — see the README for ngrok.
PLAID_WEBHOOK_URI=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Better Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs session cookies. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Changing it invalidates every existing session. Since every session here is an
# anonymous demo session, that is a cheap thing to do — rotate freely.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# The app's own origin. http://localhost:3000 locally; the deployed URL in
# production.
#
# Leave it unset for Vercel preview deployments: each one gets a fresh hostname
# that no fixed value can name, so `lib/auth` falls back to VERCEL_URL when
# VERCEL_ENV is `preview`. That fallback is deliberately gated on VERCEL_ENV —
# in production VERCEL_URL holds the deployment's own *.vercel.app URL rather
# than the custom domain, so setting this variable there is not optional.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purge job
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Bearer token authenticating `/api/purge`, which the nightly GitHub Actions
# sweep calls to delete idle demo users and remove their Plaid sandbox items.
# It is the only thing between that endpoint and the open internet, so generate
# it the same way as the secrets above and set the same value as a repository
# secret.
PURGE_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build platform (set in Vercel, NOT in .env)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Makes Vercel honour the `packageManager` pin in package.json. Without it
# Vercel infers the package manager from the lockfile version, picks pnpm 9, and
# the install fails before it starts with:
#
#   ERROR  packages field missing or empty
#
# That error names the wrong culprit. pnpm 9 reads `pnpm-workspace.yaml` as a
# workspace declaration and demands a `packages:` key; pnpm 10+ uses the same
# file for settings, which is what this repo needs it for. The file is correct,
# and adding `packages:` to satisfy pnpm 9 would only get you an install that
# silently ignores `allowBuilds` and `overrides`. The fix is this variable.
#
# It belongs in the Vercel project's Environment Variables. It is read by the
# build container, so a copy in `.env` does nothing — it is listed here only so
# the requirement is discoverable from the repo.
ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_COREPACK=1
